The bank check predicts a number someone else measured: 1,024 cycles per panel, exactly
2026-09-09. Desktop 2 (Phoenix, Ryzen 7 8700G). NO HARDWARE WAS USED and no context was taken;
this reads two build caches produced by another session and computes.

Commands (from the repo root, in the ironenv):
  python tools/aie_bank_check.py <same-bank cache>     --operands operand_a,operand_b
  python tools/aie_bank_check.py <separate-bank cache> --operands operand_a,operand_b
  python tools/aie_bank_check.py ~/.npu/cache/e9ed1c603d42d8bb153dfb76 \
      --kernel matmul_i8_i32 --operands A_L2L1_0_0_cons,B_L2L1_0_0_cons

WHY. results/aie/bank_conflict_survey.log introduced tools/aie_bank_check.py and used it to
find a same-bank paired load in the production int8 GEMM. It had never been checked against a
kernel whose penalty is actually MEASURED. The `research/windows-lowlevel` branch has one, and
it left both build caches on disk: one placement where the two operands share a bank and one
where they do not, from a single kernel source whose compiled object hash is identical in both
(983417317932ae2e...). Its measured slopes are 15,232 cycles per panel same-bank against
14,208 separate, a difference of 1,024.

That is a prediction target the tool had no part in producing.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. A BUG THE VALIDATION FOUND IMMEDIATELY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The first version of aie_bank_check.py looked for paired loads only inside HARDWARE loops --
the `.L_LEnd*` bodies whose bundle count is their cycle count. Run against the measured kernel
it reported "0 of 8 bundles" and concluded "no loop issues two loads at once; no penalty", on
the very kernel whose measured penalty is 1,024 cycles per panel.

The reason is that this kernel keeps its compute in a SOFTWARE loop. Its only hardware loop is
the trace-flush loop, eight bundles of alternating event0/event1. The same is true of mm.cc's
matmul, whose hardware loop is only the innermost k-reduction and whose accumulator-group body
is a software loop. A hardware-loop-only check is therefore wrong for both kernels this repo
cares about.

The tool now also walks software-loop bodies, taking the nearest preceding label that does not
belong to a hardware loop through to a backward branch. Branch TARGETS are unresolved
relocations in an object file, so a latch cannot be matched to its own head; where two latches
of a nest resolve to one label the shortest body is kept, so nothing is counted twice.

A second over-report was fixed at the same time. "Some bank holds two buffers" is not the
question -- a padding buffer sharing a bank with an operand is harmless, because a paired load
never reads it, and the measured kernel has exactly such a buffer named `bank2_reserve`. The
new `--operands` flag names the two buffers a paired load really reads and makes the verdict
about those alone.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. THE PLACEMENT THE TOOL READS BACK, AGAINST THE LABELS THE EXPERIMENT USED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Same kernel, same object bytes, two builds. The tool is told nothing but the cache directory
and the two operand names; the "same" and "separate" labels below are the experiment's own.

