VERDICT
  HAZARD. 1 paired-load bundle(s) in loop bodies, hardware and
  software, and a bank holds more than one buffer. If a paired load reads two
  buffers in the same bank it costs one extra cycle per iteration, so the
  loop's bundle count understates its cycles by that much.
  Predicted penalty = 1 x the iteration count of the loop it is in.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. THE PREDICTION, AND THE ARITHMETIC BEHIND IT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The tool reproduces the experiment's own labels from the ELF alone: operands in banks 1 and 2
in the build called separate, both in bank 1 in the build called same. It had no access to
those labels.

It also finds exactly ONE paired-load bundle in the compute loop's body, at 0x04aa,
  vlda wl0, [p2, #0x0] | vldb wl2, [p1, dj1]
reading the two operand pointers on the two memory ports in a single cycle.

The kernel's own source fixes the iteration count without any inference. It is a
64 x (64*panels) x 64 int8 GEMM over `aie::mmul<4, 8, 8, int8_t, int8_t, acc32>` with loop
bounds mb < 16, nb < 8, kb < 8, so one panel issues 16 x 8 x 8 = 1,024 MAC operations, and the
body carrying that paired load runs once per MAC.

  predicted penalty = 1 paired-load bundle x 1,024 iterations = 1,024 cycles per panel
  measured penalty  = 15,232 - 14,208                        = 1,024 cycles per panel

Exact, on a kernel this session did not write, against a measurement this session did not take,
from a static read of an object file. The mechanism is confirmed as SAME-BUNDLE paired loads
specifically, not two loads merely near each other: the body is eight bundles and only one of
them names both ports.

